%[text] # Image Classification with Convolutional Neural Networks
%[text] This example shows how to build, train, and evaluate convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for image classification using MATLAB. Starting from a minimal single-layer CNN and progressively improving the architecture, you will see how design choices — learning rate, depth, batch normalization, and pooling — affect classification accuracy.
%[text] The example uses the built-in handwritten digit dataset (10 classes, 28×28 grayscale images) and covers:
%[text] 1. Loading and splitting image data with `imageDatastore`.
%[text] 2. Defining a *minimal CNN* with a single convolution layer.
%[text] 3. *Tuning the learning rate* to understand its impact on convergence.
%[text] 4. Building a *deeper CNN* with multiple convolution blocks, batch normalization, and max pooling.
%[text] 5. Evaluating accuracy and visualizing predictions. \
%[text] ![](text:image:6baf)
%%
%[text] ## Load Image Data
%[text] Load the digit sample data as an image datastore. The `imageDatastore` function automatically labels the images based on folder names. The dataset has 10 classes (digits 0–9), and each image is 28×28×1 pixels.
dataFolder = fullfile(toolboxdir("nnet"), "nndemos", "nndatasets", "DigitDataset");

imds = imageDatastore(dataFolder, ...
    IncludeSubfolders=true, ...
    LabelSource="foldernames");

classNames = categories(imds.Labels);
%%
%[text] Partition the data into training, validation, and test data sets. Use 70% of the images for training, 15% for validation, and 15% for testing. Specify `"randomized"` to assign the specified proportion of files from each class to the new data sets. The `splitEachLabel` function splits the image datastore into three new datastores.
[imdsTrain, imdsValidation, imdsTest] = splitEachLabel(imds, 0.7, 0.15, 0.15, "randomized");
%%
%[text] Preview a few training images to get a sense of the data.
figure
tiledlayout("flow")
perm = randperm(numel(imdsTrain.Files), 16);
for i = 1:16
    nexttile
    img = readimage(imdsTrain, perm(i));
    imshow(img)
    title(string(imdsTrain.Labels(perm(i))))
end
sgtitle("Sample Training Images")
%%
%[text] ## Approach 1: Minimal CNN
%[text] Start with the simplest possible CNN — a single convolution layer followed by ReLU activation, a fully connected output layer, and softmax. This serves as a baseline to understand the fundamental building blocks.
%[text] ### Define Network Architecture
%[text] To build the network, use the Deep Network Designer app. 
deepNetworkDesigner
%[text] To create a blank network, pause on **Blank Network** and click **New**. 
%[text] In the **Designer** pane, define the convolutional neural network architecture. Drag layers from the **Layer Library** and connect them. To quickly search for layers, use the **Filter layers** search box in the **Layer Library** pane. To edit the properties of a layer, click the layer and edit the values in the **Properties** pane.
%[text] Drag these layers in order and connect them sequentially. First, drag an **imageInputLayer** onto the canvas and set **InputSize** to `28,28,1`.
%[text] ![](text:image:9082)
%[text] Next, drag these layers onto the canvas and connect them in order: 
%[text] - **convolution2dLayer**
%[text] - **batchNormalizationLayer**
%[text] - **reluLayer** \
%[text] Then, connect a **fullyConnectedLayer** and set **OutputSize** to the number of classes in the data, in this example, 10.
%[text] ![](text:image:1ab2)
%[text] Finally, add a **softmaxLayer**.
%[text] ![](text:image:10c5)
%[text] To check that the network is ready for training, click **Analyze**. The Deep Learning Network Analyzer reports zero errors or warnings, so the network is ready for training. To export the network, click **Export**. The app saves the network as the variable `net_1`.
%[text] ![](text:image:1a0b)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_86512BB9] ### Create Architecture without DND
net_1 = [...
    imageInputLayer([28 28 1]), ...
    convolution2dLayer(3, 32), ...
    reluLayer, ...
    fullyConnectedLayer(10), ...
    softmaxLayer];
%%
%[text] ### Train and Evaluate the Minimal CNN
%[text] Specify the training options. Choosing among the options requires empirical analysis. To explore different training option configurations by running experiments, you can use the [Experiment Manager](docid:nnet_ref#mw_11ce840f-eed7-4975-9c28-baeebd79f092) app. 
options = trainingOptions("sgdm", ...
    MaxEpochs=4, ...
    ValidationData=imdsValidation, ...
    ValidationFrequency=30, ...
    Plots="training-progress", ...
    Metrics="accuracy", ...
    Verbose=false, ...
    InitialLearnRate = 0.01); %[control:dropdown:8916]{"position":[24,28]}
%[text] ### **Train Neural Network**
%[text] Train the neural network using the [`trainnet`](docid:nnet_ref.mw_87a4761c-af20-4e6e-9752-e145a8295c97) function. Because the aim is classification, use cross-entropy loss.
net = trainnet(imdsTrain,net_1,"crossentropy",options);
%%
%[text] ### Test Neural Network
%[text] Test the neural network using the [`testnet`](docid:nnet_ref.mw_3eb84788-36da-4e3e-a351-ecbc55b86c2b) function. For single-label classification, evaluate the accuracy. The accuracy is the percentage of correct predictions. By default, the `testnet` function uses a GPU if one is available. To select the execution environment manually, use the `ExecutionEnvironment` argument of the `testnet` function.
accuracy = testnet(net,imdsTest,"accuracy")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BFA48094] ## Fine Tuning 1: Change Learning Rate and Train
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CAFA9F49] The network we train tries to minimize a loss function by using an algorithm called gradient descent. The learning rate contributes to whether we can minimize that function.
%[text] - Too high - the loss function never converges to a minimum. This is what happened when we used the default learning rate.
%[text] - Too low - the loss function converges on a local minima. \
%[text] Change the learning rate above by selecting a different value from the drop down menu. Observe the change in the accuracy
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3674D154] ## Fine Tuning 2: Improve Network Architecture and Train
%[text] Improving the network architecture can increase training accuracy. This time, we will try a more sophisticated network architecture. This network has multiple convolution layers, as well as [batch normalization](<matlab:doc batchNormalizationLayer>) layers, which improve the quality and convergence rate of the training.
net_2 = [
    imageInputLayer([28 28 1])    

    convolution2dLayer(3, 32)
    batchNormalizationLayer
    reluLayer    
    maxPooling2dLayer(5)

    convolution2dLayer(3, 64)    
    batchNormalizationLayer
    reluLayer
    maxPooling2dLayer(5)

    fullyConnectedLayer(10)
    softmaxLayer];

options = trainingOptions("sgdm", ...
    MaxEpochs=4, ...
    ValidationData=imdsValidation, ...
    ValidationFrequency=30, ...
    Plots="training-progress", ...
    Metrics="accuracy", ...
    Verbose=false, ...
    InitialLearnRate = 0.0001);

netnew = trainnet(imdsTrain, net_2, "crossentropy", options);
accuracy = testnet(netnew,imdsTest,"accuracy")
%[text] ### Food for thought
%[text] Changing the network architecture definitely had an impact of the accuracy but at the same time size of the network increased significantly. Is the improvement worth the increase in size?
%%
%[text] ### Make Predictions
%[text] Make predictions using the [`minibatchpredict`](docid:nnet_ref.mw_c7ae5ad7-5485-4270-8657-694ab264fb57) function and convert the scores to labels using the [`scores2label`](docid:nnet_ref.mw_b4669b80-206b-472f-a9eb-7e9642b3c38c) function. By default, the `minibatchpredict` function uses a GPU if one is available.
scores = minibatchpredict(net,imdsTest);
YPred = scores2label(scores,classNames);
%[text] Visualize a random sample of predictions alongside the true labels.
numTestObservations = numel(imdsTest.Files);
idx = randi(numTestObservations, 9, 1);

figure
tiledlayout("flow")
for i = 1:9
    nexttile
    img = readimage(imdsTest, idx(i));
    imshow(img)
    actual = string(imdsTest.Labels(idx(i)));
    predicted = string(YPred(idx(i)));
    title("Predicted: " + predicted)
end
sgtitle("Test Set Predictions")
%%
%[text] ### Confusion Matrix
%[text] A confusion matrix provides a detailed view of classification performance across all classes, showing which digits the network confuses most often.
figure
confusionchart(imdsTest.Labels, YPred)
title("Test Set Confusion Matrix")
%%
%[text] ## Next Steps
%[text] - **Increase depth and epochs** — add more convolution blocks and train longer for higher accuracy.
%[text] - **Data augmentation** — use `imageDataAugmenter` to apply random rotations, translations, and scaling during training, improving robustness.
%[text] - **Transfer learning** — start from a pre-trained network (e.g., ResNet, GoogLeNet) and fine-tune it on your dataset. This is especially powerful when you have limited training data.
%[text] - **Experiment Manager** — use the Experiment Manager app to systematically sweep hyperparameters and compare results. \
%%
%[text] ## Export Results
%[text] Uncomment the lines below to export this script as an HTML report.
% if ~isfolder("results"), mkdir("results"); end
